The [[Hyfman GDP Inflation Hypothesis]] is a theory by [[John Hyfman]] that claims that general GDP reporting by most [[Adonian Community]] nations are fabricated to some extant. This hypothesis was published in the [[Economic Times Journal]] on March 11, 1989 that reported that the 10 largest Adonian Community member states, including [[Zong]], [[Volisania]], [[United Midlands]], had fabricated their GDP reporting numbers to better position themselves in economic trade negotiations through the last two decades. While lower GDP did contribute to greater economic support in loan and economic development statues, larger and inflated GDP numbers allowed these nations to project power in the post-war multipolar economic climate.

The theory was well criticized by contemporaries, but the theory had substantial evidence as Hyfman had sent his students to each top ten economic power from 1980-1987 to examine visible growth versus economic growth reporting numbers. 

=== Outcome ===
Most nations had continued to report exaggerated numbers in accordance to the theory to this day, it has been proposed by contemporary economists that most nations economies are between 50-30% less productive than reported.